<article class="env-lemma" id="07JY">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07JY">Lemma <span data-tag="07JY">10.9.5</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $S \subset R$ be a multiplicative subset. The category of $S^{-1}R$-modules is equivalent to the category of $R$-modules $N$ with the property that every $s \in S$ acts as an automorphism on $N$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The functor which defines the equivalence associates to an $S^{-1}R$-module $M$ the same module but now viewed as an $R$-module via the localization map $R \to S^{-1}R$. Conversely, if $N$ is an $R$-module, such that every $s \in S$ acts via an automorphism $s_ N$, then we can think of $N$ as an $S^{-1}R$-module by letting $x/s$ act via $x_ N \circ s_ N^{-1}$. We omit the verification that these two functors are quasi-inverse to each other. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>